  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;layer is above the E layer and the ionization is less compared to E layer. It ranges to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>170</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>190</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>.</p><p>The D layer is located above the stratosphere. It ranges&nbsp;from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>&nbsp;above the earth&#39;s surface.</p><p>The troposphere starts from the ground and ranges to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>.</p><p>The E layer is located above the D layer. The intensity of ionization is less than the D layer. It ranges from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>125</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>&nbsp;above the earth&#39;s surface.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></blockquote>"
